    "result_text": "Build is green. The AUDIT log confirms the behavior: owner 11 (first household member) \u2192 `membershipLevel=1`, owner 12 (second, same household) \u2192 `membershipLevel=2` (capped at 1+1).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the household membership-level ceiling and made the suite green.\n\n**The rule** (`Owner.getMembershipLevel`, `OwnerRestControllerV1`): a new owner's derived membership level is now capped at one above the highest membership level among the other members of their household at creation time. With no existing household member, no cap applies.\n\n### Changes\n\n1. **`model/Owner.java`**\n   - Added a persistent `membershipLevelCeiling` field (nullable) with accessors.\n   - `getMembershipLevel()` now computes the level from points as before, then caps it at `membershipLevelCeiling` when one is set. Because each member's own ceiling is already applied by their `getMembershipLevel()`, capping composes correctly across a household.\n\n2. **`rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`**\n   - `addOwner` calls a new `applyMembershipLevelCeiling(owner)` before saving. It finds the non-deleted owners already sharing the new owner's `householdId`, takes the max of their current membership levels, and stores `max + 1` as the ceiling (leaving it null when the household has no other member).\n   - A helper `currentMembershipLevel` computes each existing member's level the same way a response would (populating its household member count first).\n   - **Also relaxed `applyHousehold`**: it no longer throws `DuplicateOwnerHouseholdException` when a new owner joins an existing household. This was required for the spec to be meaningful (a household must be able to hold more than one member) and it aligns the code with its own documented design \u2014 `rejectDuplicateIdentity` already states the identity key *\"subsumes the former separate telephone, email and household duplicate checks\"* and that two household members with different telephones \"are both allowed.\" The identity-key check remains the real hard-duplicate gate; explicit `sharesHousehold` acknowledgement still marks a declared member so it isn't soft-flagged as a possible duplicate. Removed the now-unused import.\n\n3. **Schema** (`db/{h2,hsqldb,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql`): added the `membership_level_ceiling` column. Updated the positional seed inserts in `db/hsqldb/data.sql` (the profile the tests run under) to include the new column; h2/postgres seed data use explicit column lists and needed no change.\n\n### Note on the household-rule change\nRelaxing the `applyHousehold` 409 goes slightly beyond the one-sentence spec, but the new acceptance test (`coreCapsLevelByHousehold`) creates a second same-household owner *without* `sharesHousehold` and expects success \u2014 so the rejection had to go for the suite to be green, and doing so resolves a pre-existing contradiction between `applyHousehold` and the documented identity-key duplicate model. `DuplicateOwnerHouseholdException` and its handler are left in place (harmless) rather than deleted. Flag this if you'd prefer the household rejection preserved instead.",
